# Lesson 4, new example hand: the lesson's first layout (AQJT75 opposite 642) as trumps, finessing twice from dummy.
title: Lead from dummy toward your tenace
id: L04-01
type: example
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.1, 3.5
student: S
board: 55
dealer: S
vulnerable: both

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  642    K85    K94    9632
  E  K83    JT73   652    A54
  S  AQJT75 Q4     A83    KQ
  W  9      A962   QJT7   JT87

auction:
  S 1S     = 13+ points, at least four spades
  W Pass
  N 2S     = 6-9 points, 3+ spades
  E Pass
  S 4S     = Game
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♠ with 18 points and a good six-card suit. Partner raised to
  2♠, and with that much strength you went straight to game. You are
  declarer, because you bid spades first, and so West will lead to the first
  trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 4
  spades: 1 = The king, unless East has it and you can lead toward your ace-queen.
  hearts: 1 = The ace. After it's gone, dummy's king and your queen are both good.
  diamonds: 1 = Your third diamond.
  clubs: 1 = The ace.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♠. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: Which loser might go away? Where would the lead have to be for that?
play:
  1   W DQ  N D4  E D2  S DA
      note: Four losers, one too many. The one that might go away is the ♠K. If East has it, a spade led from dummy puts East on the spot: whatever East plays, you play just enough to beat it. Declarer wins this trick in hand and keeps dummy's ♦K as a way to get there.
      pause
  2   S D3  W D7  N DK  E D5
      note: Over to dummy with the ♦K, so the first spade comes from dummy.
  3   N S2  E S3  S SQ  W S9
      note: The finesse. East plays low, so declarer plays the queen. If West had the king, the queen would lose to it, but East has it, so the queen wins.
      pause
  4   S H4  W H2  N HK  E H3
      note: East may still hold the king, guarded. To lead spades from dummy again, declarer needs another way there: a heart toward dummy's king. West has the ace but plays low, so the king wins.
  5   N S4  E S8  S SJ  W C7
      note: Lead from dummy again. East plays low, and the jack wins.
  6   S SA  W C8  N S6  E SK
      note: Now the ace, and East's king drops. All the trumps are drawn, and you have lost no spade at all.
  7   S CK  W CT  N C2  E CA
      note: Now give up the tricks you have to lose. First the ♣A.
  8   E HJ  S HQ  W HA  N H5
  9   W DJ  N D9  E D6  S D8
  10  W DT  N C3  E H7  S S5
  11  S CQ  W CJ  N C9  E C4
      note: The rest are yours.
  12  S S7  W H9  N C6  E C5
  13  S ST  W H6  N H8  E HT
      note: 10 tricks: 4♠ made.

play recap:
  The plan: you counted four losers, one too many, and saw that the ♠K was
  the one that might go away. It would, if East held it and you led spades
  from dummy.

  That is a finesse: you lead toward the cards you want to win with, and
  wait to see what the opponent in between plays. You needed to lead from
  dummy twice, because East's king was guarded twice, so you kept the ♦K
  and the ♥K as ways to get there. If West had held the king, the finesse
  would have lost, and you would have been no worse off than if you had
  never tried.
